\code{log(1 + n)}, close to differences of \code{log(n)} when counts are large.

\strong{Seasonality.} The seasonal component is a Fourier regression with
period \code{period} and \code{t} the number of weeks since 1970-01-01 (as in
\code{\link[=extract_trend]{extract_trend()}}). The number of harmonics \code{K} is chosen once per key
and pass by the AICc of the \code{smooth} Kalman model (\code{\link[=extract_trend]{extract_trend()}} with
\code{method = "smooth"}, on the series with the excluded weeks set to \code{NA}),
or fixed by \code{harmonics}, and is shared by every method, so that methods
differ only in the trend filter. The seasonal coefficients are estimated
by backfitting (\code{n_backfit} iterations, as in \code{\link[=extract_trend]{extract_trend()}}) with the
trend filter of the method, except \code{gam_ar1}, which has the harmonics in
its formula.

\strong{Methods} (each smoothing parameter is chosen from the data):
\itemize{
\item \code{henderson}: \code{\link[=henderson_filter]{henderson_filter()}} with the length chosen by GCV among
13, 23 and 39 (lengths longer than the series are skipped) and the
Musgrave end weights with the I/C ratio of a preliminary 13-term pass.
Missing weeks of the input are linearly interpolated; excluded weeks
are replaced by the fitted values (trend + seasonal) of the previous
pass (X-11 style).
\item \code{loess}: \code{stats::loess(degree = 1, family = "symmetric")} on time,
with the span chosen on the grid \verb{0.06, 0.08, ..., 0.50} by the GCV of
the gaussian fit, \verb{n RSS / (n - trace.hat)^2}; \code{surface = "direct"} so
that the trend extends to missing weeks at the ends.
\item \code{gam_ar1}: \code{mgcv::bam(y ~ s(t, bs = "cr", k = 40) + Fourier, rho, AR.start, method = "fREML")} (\code{k} reduced on short series), with
\code{rho} on the grid \verb{0, 0.05, ..., 0.9} chosen at the minimum fREML
score; \code{AR.start} marks the first week after each missing stretch. The
trend is the intercept plus the spline, \code{trend_se} its standard error
from the Bayesian covariance matrix. Requires mgcv.
\item \code{whittaker}: \code{\link[=whittaker_smooth]{whittaker_smooth()}} of order 2 with \eqn{\lambda} by
GCV; missing weeks get weight zero.
\item \code{level}: Kalman local level, \code{stats::StructTS(type = "level")} and
\code{stats::KalmanSmooth()}, by maximum likelihood.
\item \code{llt}, \code{smooth}: the local linear trend and the smooth trend model
(level variance 0) of \code{\link[=extract_trend]{extract_trend()}}, by maximum likelihood.
\item \code{smooth_t}: robust Kalman smoother. It starts from the \code{smooth} ML fit
(seasonal component, observation variance \eqn{\hat h}, signal-to-noise
ratio \code{q = var_slope / var_obs}), then iterates (at most 20 times) a
Kalman filter and Rauch-Tung-Striebel smoother of the same model with
